│
├── partitioning
├── ordering
├── event streams
├── consumer groups
├── idempotency
├── replay
├── failure recovery
└── eventual consistency

Databases
│
├── PostgreSQL
├── transactions
├── indexes
├── locking
├── isolation
└── durable state

Infrastructure
│
├── Docker
├── Kafka
├── Redis
└── observability

Frontend
│
├── React
├── TypeScript
├── WebSockets
└── real-time state

Engineering
│
├── testing
├── benchmarking
├── profiling
├── architecture
├── ADRs
└── technical documentation
```

---

# Final Principle

**Do not optimize for the number of technologies in the README.**

Optimize for the number of difficult engineering questions the project forces you to solve.

A small system that correctly handles:

```text
10,000 concurrent orders
        ↓
deterministic matching
        ↓
durable event stream
        ↓
duplicate delivery
        ↓
service crash
        ↓
event replay
        ↓
correct recovered state
```

is substantially more impressive than a huge repository containing:

```text
React
Next.js
Go
Python
Kafka
RabbitMQ
Redis
MongoDB
PostgreSQL
Kubernetes
Terraform
AWS
AI
```

with no clear reason why any of them exist.

**ABADAR should grow from a correct matching engine into a distributed system—not from a collection of technologies into a matching engine.**

That progression is the project.
